- Story & Context: Under the scorching noon heat of the 1975 San Juan blackout, radio insurgent Mateo Cruz pried open the junction cabinet on Avenida Ponce de León and spliced a hand-cranked music box into the municipal grid. As the muted brass line kicked in over the pirate FM frequency, a crowd of striking transit workers began to sway in the humid air, turning an industrial protest into an impromptu street party. The track captures that exact moment when civil disobedience softened into a sun-drenched, modal groove, leaving the local police baffled by the sweet lullaby humming from the traffic lights. "When the grid goes dark, the tines will sing us home," whispers the opening vocal loop over a fluttering tape-delay shift.
